WEBVTT

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:10.180
Every mark on these sheets was made by somebody reading. So what did they keep? Let's find out, with a book short enough to read in thirty seconds.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:17.800
First, lowercase everything. And the full stops and exclamation marks count as words too.

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:31.820
Now read it two words at a time. Find the row for the first word. The second word goes in a box on that row, with a mark beside it. Then slide along by one, and do it again.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:44.180
A new word after the same word takes the next empty box. And when a pair comes round again, it gets another mark. By the end, "the fence" has six marks, and "the magpie" five.

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:51.320
That's all training is. Here's the rest of the book, done at the speed a computer does it.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:01:01.700
Now look at what it kept. Not the sentences. Not the story. It doesn't know what a magpie is. Just counts of what came next.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:09.560
And a row of marks is just a row of numbers. The row for "the" says five, six, four, four.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:17.160
When people talk about a model's "weights", this is the kind of thing they mean: numbers that came from the text it read.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:26.800
Give it a bigger book and nothing changes. This is Frankenstein. Eighty-five thousand words, and seven thousand rows.

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:36.020
A real model reads trillions of words, and stores its numbers more cleverly than a ledger. But the job it's doing is the one you're about to do.

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:53.420
Three things catch people out. A full stop is a word, and its row says how sentences start. A new word goes in the next empty box, never skip one. And say a new word out loud when you start its row, so nobody starts it twice.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:58.680
Reader, grab the text. Two words at a time.
